  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 325 PM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\n***** HEAT AND HUMIDITY THROUGH THE WEEKEND *****\n\nThe main weather impact for the weekend will be temperatures warming \ninto the mid/upper 90s as upper level ridging expands northward over \nthe Ohio Valley into the Great Lakes and the stalled frontal \nboundary currently over central IN lifts northward towards northern \nIN and southern MI tomorrow. Because of this, a second Heat Advisory \nwas issued for Saturday morning into Sunday evening for most of the \nCWA with heat index values up 108 degrees when you factor in \ndewpoint values into the mid/upper 70s both Saturday and Sunday. \n\nThe main limiting factor to the heat, especially across the \nBluegrass and our far eastern CWA will be cloud cover and the \npotential for showers and thunderstorms especially on Saturday. \nModels continue to highlight an MCS over IA and northern IL at the \nstart of the day tomorrow. This activity is then forecast to work \nalong the boundary and dive southward out of eastern IN towards \nCincinnati in the afternoon extending into the far northeastern \nportion of our CWA. The other thing we would need to watch will be \nany outflow boundaries that form and work southward into southern \nIN. These boundaries could be enough lift to fire off some limited \nconvection. SPC does have portions of our CWA, Harrison and Nicholas \ncounties under a Slight Risk or level 2 out of 5 for severe weather \nas there will be a little better bulk shear for possible gusty winds \nand hail with a Marginal risk extending back to the west from \nLouisville down to Somerset. Overall confidence in severe and even \nstorm threat remains low. \n\nThe next potential threat for storms will come with an approaching \nboundary Sunday night into early Monday morning. Timing is still in \nquestion but we do have an SPC Day 3 Marginal Outlook for central KY \nand southern IN with the main threat being strong gusty winds. Due \nto the timing questions, confidence is rather low at this time but \ndoes need to be monitored as we go through the first part of the \nweekend. \n\n***** LESS HOT, MORE STORMY TO KICK OFF NEXt WORK WEEK *****\n\nA shortwave trough looks to flatten the ridge late Sunday into \nMonday, bringing better (40-70%) chances for storms as early as \nSunday night, and perhaps offering at least limited relief from the \noppressive heat and humidity Monday into Tuesday. WPC Cluster \nanalysis suggests that, by midweek, the highest source of \nuncertainty among long range ensembles (given by PC1 for EOF1) is in \nthe east-west placement of h5 ridging building over the central US \nand hence: whether our area sees (outcome 1) oppressive heat return \nor (outcome 2) remains far enough northeast of the ridge to instead \nsee more seasonable temperatures and waves of \"ridge-riding\" \nthunderstorms. About 50% of models favor each scenario.",
